Okay, FWIW ... MS-DOS was a set of system services 
for single-tasking programs that used 20-bit segmented 
memory addressing and used the machine BIOS to run 
the screen in character mode.  The machine BIOS is 
non-reentrant and therefore cannot be used for a 
multitasking system.

The MSDOS port of nethack is for that platform. It is 
not intended to run in the multitasking 32-bit Microsoft 
operating systems known collectively as Windows.  The 
fact that it does so is due to a clumsy simulation of 
the MSDOS environment inside 32-bit windows.  The 
simulation has very poor performance.  The fact that it
works at all is a little bit remarkable, but not 
interesting from a development point of view because 
making a real 32-bit windows port is easy and gives 
much better performance. 

Several editions of 32-bit windows have a command-line
interface which emulates the MSDOS platform, but they 
(obviously) cannot use the machine BIOS to run the 
screen in character mode or to get keyboard events 
directly, and the 20-bit segmented memory addressing 
mode used in MSDOS is not how windows maps its 
memory either, so it all has to be simulated.  Every 
single memory access made by a MSDOS program running 
in this environment has to be translated into a 32-bit 
non-segmented address, checked against machine BIOS 
addresses that windows can't allow programs to use, 
and trapped to the correct emulator routine if it 
happens to be a BIOS write, read, or call, etc.   

Memory address space translation and trap costs aside, 
the emulator routines are much less efficient than the 
original machine BIOS.  For example, writing bitmap 
pictures of characters to pixel coordinates and providing 
(and calling) a host of window-maintenance routines rather 
than writing character codes into BIOS screen memory at 
character coordinates and being done with it.  Windows 
thus virtualizes single-tasking access to the machine 
BIOS in a protected, multitasking environment. 

Unfortunately, it does so rather clumsily.  The result 
is that the MSDOS port, although it can be run in those 
command-line environments under 32-bit windows, runs 
very slowly.

To address this problem, the devteam created a 
Windows port of Nethack, which uses the services 
available in 32-bit windows and uses its preferred 
memory addressing modes, requiring no emulation or 
virtualization.  The windows version runs in a 32-bit
windows environment with reasonably snappy response 
time.  I recommend using it.

I also recommend NOT claiming anymore that Windows is 
MSDOS.  It makes you look silly and ignorant.  Although 
both are "disk operating systems" created by microsoft, 
the runtime environment for programs is completely 
different and MSDOS programs run under Windows only 
by costly and slow emulation. 

